{"text": "Lesson seven, pillar four, hardware and systems. The 1st three pillars specify a cheaper model. This pillar collects waste they cannot see, bytes that move more than once, and compute that stalls waiting. It is the only pillar that hits all four bottlenecks, and the only one that is mostly lossless. A request served by FlashAttention on a paged cache produces the same bits as a naive run. You pay engineering complexity, not perplexity. Textbook SoftMax writes a full attention matrix into high bandwidth memory. The bytes dominate the arithmetic. FlashAttention tiles queries, keys, and values through on-chip memory, runs SoftMax online, and writes only the output. It is exact, not an approximation, often two to four times faster. The 3rd version adds eight-bit floats and reaches about three-quarters of an H100's peak. Those kernels were shaped for training, where queries, keys, and values have similar lengths. Decode has one query against a long cache. FlashInfer targets serving paged layouts, ragged batches, and the attention variance used in production. The most important systems idea in the survey: manage the cache like virtual memory, reserving one contiguous slot out to the maximum length fragments memory, because requests die early. PagedAttention allocates fixed pages, typically 16 tokens, and a block table per request. The outputs do not change, only the wasted reservation does. Continuous batching is the matching scheduler. Do not wait for the longest request. Step every one once, and admit new work at the boundary. Shared prefixes should not be pre-filled twice. System prompts, few-shot examples, and chat history. RadixAttention stores cache entries in a tree and evicts the cold ones. A long pre-fill on the same GPU as in-flight decodes stalls every user and blows up time per output token. DistServe Splitwise and Mooncake split the phases onto different pools. DistServe optimizes goodput under a latency target, Splitwise puts compute-heavy GPUs on prefill and bandwidth-heavy GPUs on decode, Mooncake adds a global cache pool, Sarathi gets much of the benefit without a 2nd pool by chunking prefill and interleaving it with decode. The open stacks have converged on paging, continuous batching, FlashAttention, and prefix caches. What still differs is scheduling, kernel quality, and how cleanly a stack joins a disaggregated topology.", "language": "en", "duration": 154.608, "words": [{"text": "Lesson", "start": 0.14, "end": 0.46}, {"text": "seven,", "start": 0.6, "end": 0.92}, {"text": "pillar", "start": 1.501, "end": 1.781}, {"text": "four,", "start": 1.861, "end": 2.121}, {"text": "hardware", "start": 2.461, "end": 2.921}, {"text": "and", "start": 2.941, "end": 3.041}, {"text": "systems.", "start": 3.101, "end": 3.581}, {"text": "The", "start": 4.261, "end": 4.341}, {"text": "first", "start": 4.401, "end": 4.642}, {"text": "three", "start": 4.682, "end": 4.862}, {"text": "pillars", "start": 4.902, "end": 5.262}, {"text": "specify", "start": 5.322, "end": 5.822}, {"text": "a", "start": 5.882, "end": 5.902}, {"text": "cheaper", "start": 5.982, "end": 6.282}, {"text": "model.", "start": 6.342, "end": 6.642}, {"text": "This", "start": 7.222, "end": 7.382}, {"text": "pillar", "start": 7.462, "end": 7.703}, {"text": "collects", "start": 7.763, "end": 8.103}, {"text": "waste", "start": 8.183, "end": 8.483}, {"text": "they", "start": 8.503, "end": 8.603}, {"text": "cannot", "start": 8.643, "end": 8.943}, {"text": "see,", "start": 9.083, "end": 9.283}, {"text": "bytes", "start": 9.963, "end": 10.263}, {"text": "that", "start": 10.283, "end": 10.403}, {"text": "move", "start": 10.463, "end": 10.664}, {"text": "more", "start": 10.744, "end": 10.904}, {"text": "than", "start": 10.904, "end": 11.044}, {"text": "once,", "start": 11.204, "end": 11.484}, {"text": "and", "start": 11.924, "end": 12.024}, {"text": "compute", "start": 12.064, "end": 12.504}, {"text": "that", "start": 12.524, "end": 12.644}, {"text": "stalls", "start